WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.970
Netflix has rapidly become a ubiquitous part
of nearly all of our lives, with its incredibly

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.320
deep, constantly shifting, streaming catalog
of movies and television series.

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:14.800
Usually, the phrase "there's something for
everyone" is mere hyperbole, but not in the

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:20.180
case of Netflix, which absolutely has something
for you whether you're a fan of extreme horror,

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.940
an actual toddler, or anything in between.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:27.420
Of course, with such an extensive selection,
there's bound to be some content that's a

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:28.500
bit problematic.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:34.080
Whether it's the material itself, the production's
background, or inappropriate scenes, Netflix

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.380
has put up a surprising amount of material
that's drawn controversy for one reason or

00:00:38.380 --> 00:00:38.880
another.

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:42.260
Here's a look at some of Netflix's most controversial
offerings.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:44.640
13 Reasons Why

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:51.420
When 13 Reasons Why debuted in 2017, it immediately
became a lightning rod for controversy.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.500
The story picks up after high schooler Hannah
Baker takes her own life, leaving behind a

00:00:56.500 --> 00:00:59.840
series of cassette tapes that details the
reasons for her actions.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:05.920
Via flashbacks, the show depicts disturbing
instances of assault, bullying, and Hannah's

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:06.980
own final moments.

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.780
Despite its seemingly self-contained premise,
the series was picked up for a second season,

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.820
providing a fresh round of controversy in
2018.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:20.860
The new episodes' more problematic scenes
include an explicit depiction of a young man's

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:24.960
assault by another man, an incident that nearly
prompts a school shooting.

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:29.200
To its credit, Netflix has added content warnings
before every episode.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:34.039
There's also now a companion series, Beyond
the Reasons, to encourage discussions of the

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:35.500
issues the show presents.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:38.580
But some continue to label it mere exploitation.

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.020
Blue is the Warmest Color

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.880
2013 French drama Blue Is the Warmest Color
won the Palme d'Or at the Cannes Film Festival,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.740
despite drawing controversy for its shockingly
graphic, seven-minute long lesbian love scene.

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:58.860
Comments from lead actresses Adèle Exarchopoulos
and Léa Seydoux concerning the film's production,

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.600
and director Abdellatif Kechiche's reaction
to them, have only added to the controversy.

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.950
In a Daily Beast interview, Exarchopoulos
and Seydoux praised Kechiche as a genius.

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.280
But they also called the making of the film
"horrible" and insisted that neither would

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:15.610
work with him again, with Seydoux saying,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.860
"In America, we’d all be in jail."

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:23.000
Kechiche then took his stars to task for their
comments, even threatening to take them to

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:23.500
court.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.800
The film is undeniably brilliant, but with
this context, it's tough not to see its more

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:30.520
demanding scenes in a new light.

00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:32.440
Atypical

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:37.780
Building a wacky sitcom around a main character
with high-functioning autism is nothing if

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:38.460
not tricky.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.860
Atypical has its heart in the right place,
but it also hits every stumbling block along

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:43.360
the way.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:48.360
It can be forgiven for failing to put together
a complete and accurate picture of the condition,

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:52.900
as there are far too many variations along
the spectrum for one character to embody them

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:53.400
all.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.780
But some have taken the show to task for attempting
to play up the condition for laughs, while

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:00.380
still failing to be insightful, or funny.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.000
"I'm doing research on how to steal a woman
and Bailey is the perfect test subject because

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:07.840
she's a skank."

00:03:08.210 --> 00:03:12.580
Outlets like Huffington Post and Salon have
slapped the show with labels ranging from

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:14.700
"stereotypical" to "offensive."

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:19.840
Creator Robia Rashid and her team have also
inspired criticism for failing to consult

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:21.840
any actual autistic people.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:26.620
But the series seems to have found its fanbase,
as Netflix has renewed it for a second season;

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.260
perhaps with a little course correction, it
can become the shining example of mainstream

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.040
representation that the autism community was
hoping for.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.280
For now, it's just more of this:

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:37.740
"Bazinga."

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:38.840
Love

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.680
Gaspar Noé seems to live to provoke controversy.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.230
The mind behind such famously challenging
cinematic experiments as Enter the Void and

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:54.030
Irreversible, Noe used his 2015 film Love
to tell the fairly simple story of the birth,

00:03:54.030 --> 00:03:56.480
life, and death of a romantic relationship.

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:00.700
But Noé being Noé, he told it in a fashion
that's anything but simple.

00:04:01.160 --> 00:04:05.680
Specifically, he included unsimulated lovemaking
and shot it all in 3D.

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:11.100
In case you're wondering, yes, there is an
explosive shot that takes full advantage of

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:12.560
the 3D format.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.140
"No, God!"

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.260
"No, God, please no!"

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:24.760
It's a supremely silly moment in what is otherwise
a rather serious and grounded film.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:31.340
Critics generally agreed that Love was underwhelming,
self-indulgent and strangely un-erotic.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:36.419
Even so, it's without a doubt the closest
thing you'll find to hardcore content on Netflix.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:37.480
If that's your thing.

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:38.560
We're not judging.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:40.000
Big Mouth

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:45.719
Netflix has generally fared quite well with
its original animated offerings, with titles

00:04:45.719 --> 00:04:51.400
like BoJack Horseman and F is for Family soaking
up critical acclaim despite their adult themes

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:51.900
and humor.

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.680
But with Big Mouth, they took a considerable
risk.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.140
An examination of puberty, the show leans
heavily on vulgarity and gross-out humor.

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:04.520
While some critics have found it to be sharp,
funny and engaging, others have deemed its

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:07.300
utter commitment to nastiness a bridge too
far.

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.550
The criticism ranges from supposed confusion
over whether the show is aimed at children,

00:05:12.550 --> 00:05:18.200
to its alleged liberal agenda, and sideways
references to homosexuality and things.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:24.030
The series has also managed to draw ire for
casting Jenny Slate, a white actress, as the

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:25.340
voice of a black character.

00:05:25.620 --> 00:05:30.020
But despite all of the brouhaha, the first
season was a significant hit with viewers,

00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:32.620
and Netflix will be back with more for season
2.

00:05:35.060 --> 00:05:37.320
"So, did you guys hear the news?"

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.380
Nymphomaniac

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.480
Professional provocateur Lars Von Trier's
Nymphomaniac arrived in two feature-length

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:45.980
volumes.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.280
It's the story of a woman recounting her devastating
romantic history to a seemingly sympathetic

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.580
older man after she's brutally beaten.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.480
Nymphomaniac is not so much titillating as
it is psychologically uncomfortable.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:03.440
Of course, it's the lovemaking scenes that
stirred up the most controversy, causing the

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.120
film to be banned in multiple countries.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.960
But if it's eroticism you're looking for,
you won't find it here.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:15.080
Nymphomaniac is an unforgiving portrait of
how its main character's compulsions inevitably

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.980
ruin her life and the lives of nearly everyone
she comes into contact with.

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:23.400
And sadly, there's no redemption to be found
in its jaw-dropping ending.

00:06:27.440 --> 00:06:28.140
The Push

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:34.160
Netflix's original special The Push stars
English mentalist Derren Brown, who wowed

00:06:34.169 --> 00:06:39.340
viewers with a viral video in which he demonstrated
the seeming ability to literally put thoughts

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:40.240
into people's heads.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:45.720
This involved convincing actor Simon Pegg
that he had always wanted a red BMX bike as

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:46.220
a child.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.500
I don't know what's going on. What's my name?  He's going to have me in a pickle jar by 2.

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:52.160
I know it

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:58.020
But in The Push, he enlists the help of over
70 actors to attempt to put a very different

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.240
kind of idea into the mind of an unsuspecting
mark.

00:07:01.640 --> 00:07:07.000
The actors guide an ordinary man named Chris
through a series of increasingly bizarre situations.

00:07:07.420 --> 00:07:12.000
Ultimately Chris must decide, under intense
pressure, whether to push another man from

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.280
the top of a tall building.

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:18.020
Brown isn't actually trying to get Chris to
commit murder, just to convince him of the

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:18.580
idea.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:24.100
But that's a distinction that walks a very
fine line between bizarre prank and psychological

00:07:24.160 --> 00:07:24.660
torture.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:36.100
The Paperboy

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.780
2012's The Paperboy is the kind of quirky,
tonally challenged film that might have come

00:07:41.789 --> 00:07:46.610
and gone in limited release with few having
noticed, despite the presence of bankable

00:07:46.610 --> 00:07:50.560
stars Nicole Kidman, Matthew McConaughey,
and Zac Efron.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:55.160
Though it features stylish direction from
Lee Daniels, this melodramatic thriller is

00:07:55.160 --> 00:07:59.940
kind of a mess and mostly unmemorable, except
for for one truty weird scene.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:04.600
After Efron suffers a jellyfish sting, Kidman
attempts to alleviate the pain by urinating

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:05.100
on him.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:10.880
And that may not even be the film's most disturbing
scene, thanks to the bizarre prison hanky-panky

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:12.940
between Kidman and John Cusack.

00:08:13.440 --> 00:08:18.260
But it's just not every day that you get to
see major movie stars peeing on each other,

00:08:18.260 --> 00:08:21.560
and that was pretty much all anyone could
talk about leading up to the film's release.

00:08:22.620 --> 00:08:24.120
"I don't even know what I expected"

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:25.740
Step Sisters

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:31.900
Netflix's acquisition of Step Sisters, a topical
exploration of race on college campuses from

00:08:31.909 --> 00:08:36.250
Dear White People writer Chuck Hayward and
Master of None's Lena Waithe, seemed like

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:36.880
a no-brainer.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:42.090
But the movie's central premise, in which
a black college senior is tasked with teaching

00:08:42.090 --> 00:08:46.880
a bunch of disgraced white sorority sisters
to step, sparked controversy before it even

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:47.380
debuted.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:52.340
The film's creators have taken issue with
Step Sisters being pigeonholed as a "cultural

00:08:52.340 --> 00:08:53.380
appropriation comedy."

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:57.640
Waithe has pointed out that it has a high
degree of self-awareness and that

00:08:57.640 --> 00:09:00.640
"Culture is meant to be appreciated and explored
by everyone."

00:09:01.070 --> 00:09:05.540
Many critics have also panned the film for
the more conventional crimes of awkward plotting,

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:07.690
recycled tropes, and dull dialogue.

00:09:07.880 --> 00:09:12.540
At a time when race relations is a hot topic
in the United States, it seems unlikely that

00:09:12.540 --> 00:09:16.180
anyone involved in the production could have
failed to foresee the flap that it would cause.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:17.920
White Girl

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:24.220
Writer/director Elizabeth Wood leaned on a
couple of very specific influences for her

00:09:24.220 --> 00:09:30.840
2016 indie film White Girl: writer Harmony
Korine and director Larry Clark's 1995 provocative

00:09:30.840 --> 00:09:35.780
Kids, and her own past as a hard-partying
girl from the Midwest attending college in

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:36.280
New York.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:41.400
White Girl is concerned with matters of power
and control, as fresh-faced protagonist Leah

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:46.920
loses both as she delves deeper into a lifestyle
of illicit drugs and casual relationships.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:52.060
After the film's debut at the Sundance Film
Festival, Wood was accused of playing to the

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:55.240
exploitation crowd in order to shoulder her
way into Hollywood.

00:09:55.650 --> 00:09:58.460
In an interview with Vogue, she responded
by saying,

00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:03.230
"It's interesting to me that sexuality upsets
people more than talking about race, or privilege,

00:10:03.230 --> 00:10:03.780
or gender."

